namespace Death {
//###==##====#=====--==~--~=~- --- -- -  -  -   -

/** @page multiplayer Multiplayer
@brief Guide how to run, configure and join multiplayer servers

@tableofcontents

@m_class{m-noindent}

Multiplayer comes in two flavors, both of them optional at build time:

-   **Local splitscreen** @m_span{m-text m-dim} (`WITH_MULTIPLAYER`) @m_endspan --- two to four players
    on a single machine, no networking involved. Every game mode can be played this way.
-   **Online multiplayer** @m_span{m-text m-dim} (`WITH_ONLINE_MULTIPLAYER`, and `WITH_WEBSOCKET` for the
    additional WebSocket transport) @m_endspan --- players connect over a network to a server, which can be
    the game itself or a headless dedicated server.

@m_class{m-noindent}

Every online session is **server-authoritative**: one side simulates the level, decides about damage,
collectibles, scores and round transitions, and everyone else sends input and receives state. Because of
that, a server is not a passive relay --- it runs the whole game and the level has to be present on it. The
role a machine plays is decided when the session starts, so the same executable is both the game and the
server unless it was built with `DEDICATED_SERVER`. Multiplayer also requires threads
@m_span{m-text m-dim} (`NCINE_WITH_THREADS`) @m_endspan, so it's compiled out on the few platforms that
have none, see @ref building-config-params-game.

@section multiplayer-hosting Running a server

@subsection multiplayer-hosting-listen Hosting from the game

Choose **Play Multiplayer** in the main menu and then one of:

-   **Create Public Server** --- the server announces itself, see @ref multiplayer-visibility
-   **Create Private Server** --- the server stays hidden and can only be joined by address
-   **Create Local Splitscreen Game** --- no networking at all, the additional players use gamepads
    or a shared keyboard on the same machine

@m_class{m-noindent}

The next screens select the level (or the configured playlist) and the game mode. The host plays as an
ordinary player, but its process is the authority for everyone else, which also means it has to be reachable
--- see @ref multiplayer-visibility for the port to forward. Everything else comes from the very same
configuration file the dedicated server reads @m_span{m-text m-dim} (@cpp "Jazz2.Server.config" @ce, see
@ref multiplayer-config) @m_endspan, only the game mode, the level and the public/private choice are taken
from the menu instead of from the file.

@subsection multiplayer-hosting-dedicated Running a dedicated server

A dedicated server is the same executable started with the `/server` argument, optionally followed by the
path to its configuration file:

@code{.sh}
./jazz2 /server Jazz2.Server.config
@endcode

@m_class{m-noindent}

In this mode the application opens no window and initializes neither graphics nor audio, runs the simulation
at a fixed rate of 60 frames per second, and reads console commands from standard input, so it can be run
over SSH, from a service manager or in a container. The argument is available on **Linux**, **macOS** and ---
in a debug build only --- on **Windows**; a release server for Windows is the `DEDICATED_SERVER` build, where
the executable is always a server and takes the configuration path as its **first argument** without
`/server`. See @ref building-config-params-game for both build parameters.

@m_class{m-noindent}

A dedicated server needs the same game files as a normal installation --- the original **Jazz Jackrabbit 2**
data in the @cpp "Source" @ce directory, which it converts into @cpp "Cache" @ce on the first start --- and
it needs a **playlist**: the level it starts with is taken from the current playlist entry, so a
configuration without one is refused with *"Server cannot be started because of invalid configuration"*. The
configuration path is relative to the directory of the preferences file @m_span{m-text m-dim} (the same
directory @cpp "Jazz2.config" @ce lives in, which `/config` can move elsewhere) @m_endspan; an absolute path
is used as it is, and if the argument is omitted, @cpp "Jazz2.Server.config" @ce is loaded from there.

@m_class{m-noindent}

Everything typed on standard input is sent to the server: a line starting with `/` is a command
@m_span{m-text m-dim} (see @ref multiplayer-commands) @m_endspan, anything else is broadcast as a chat
message from the server. `/exit` or `/quit` stops the server, and so do `Ctrl+C` and a termination signal
@m_span{m-text m-dim} (`SIGINT` and `SIGTERM`; on Windows the console close events) @m_endspan --- all of
them shut it down cleanly, disconnecting the peers and delisting the server from the online list before the
process exits. A second signal during an already running shutdown terminates the process the usual way.

@subsection multiplayer-hosting-container Running a dedicated server as a container

The dedicated server is also published as a multi-architecture container image, which needs nothing to be
built and comes with the `DEDICATED_SERVER` configuration already applied. See @ref building-docker for the
image, the volumes it expects and the `docker-compose.yml` that describes the whole setup, including running
several instances on a single host.

@section multiplayer-config Configuring a server

@subsection multiplayer-config-file Configuration file

The server is configured by a single JSON file, by default @cpp "Jazz2.Server.config" @ce next to the
preferences file. Comments are allowed in it, both the block and the single-line form, and the
@cpp "$include" @ce directive merges another file into it @m_span{m-text m-dim} (recursively, but each file
only once, so an include loop is harmless) @m_endspan --- which is how several instances can share their
common settings and only differ in a few properties. A minimal configuration of a server that rotates two
levels looks like this:

@code{.json}
{
	"ServerName": "{PlayerName}'s Server",
	"ServerPort": 7438,
	"MaxPlayerCount": 16,
	"MinPlayerCount": 2,

	"Playlist": [
		{ "LevelName": "battle1", "GameMode": "battle", "TotalKills": 15 },
		{ "LevelName": "arace1", "GameMode": "race", "TotalLaps": 3 }
	],
	"PlaylistIndex": 0
}
@endcode

@m_class{m-noindent}

See @ref Jazz2::Multiplayer::ServerConfiguration for the description of **every** supported property,
their default values and a fully annotated example configuration. The properties fall into a few groups:

-   **Identity and reachability** --- @cpp "ServerName" @ce, @cpp "ServerPort" @ce,
    @cpp "ServerAddressOverride" @ce, @cpp "IsPrivate" @ce and the WebSocket transport
    @m_span{m-text m-dim} (@cpp "WsPort" @ce, @cpp "WsCertPath" @ce, @cpp "WsKeyPath" @ce) @m_endspan
-   **Access control** --- @cpp "ServerPassword" @ce, @cpp "MaxPlayerCount" @ce,
    @cpp "AllowedPlayerTypes" @ce, @cpp "RequiresDiscordAuth" @ce and the player ID lists, see
    @ref multiplayer-config-access
-   **Rules of a round** --- @cpp "GameMode" @ce, @cpp "InitialPlayerHealth" @ce, @cpp "TotalKills" @ce,
    @cpp "TotalLaps" @ce, @cpp "TotalTreasureCollected" @ce, @cpp "MaxGameTimeSecs" @ce,
    @cpp "Elimination" @ce, the team properties and the gameplay switches such as
    @cpp "ReforgedGameplay" @ce, @cpp "AllowLedgeClimb" @ce or @cpp "PlayerStacking" @ce
-   **Session flow** --- @cpp "MinPlayerCount" @ce and @cpp "PreGameSecs" @ce decide when a round starts,
    @cpp "TotalPlayerPoints" @ce how long a championship of several rounds lasts,
    @cpp "AllowJoinDuringRound" @ce, @cpp "JoinCooldownSecs" @ce, @cpp "ReconnectWindowSecs" @ce and
    @cpp "IdleKickTimeSecs" @ce how players enter and leave it
-   **Operations** --- @cpp "AllowAssetStreaming" @ce lets clients download levels they don't have from
    the server, and @cpp "WebhookUrl" @ce with @cpp "WebhookEvents" @ce post selected server events to a
    **Discord** channel

@m_class{m-noindent}

@cpp "{PlayerName}" @ce and @cpp "{ServerName}" @ce can be used in @cpp "ServerName" @ce and
@cpp "WelcomeMessage" @ce, and both properties also support @ref Jazz2-UI-Font-format "text formatting".

@subsection multiplayer-config-playlist Playlist

@cpp "Playlist" @ce is an array of rounds, each of them at least a level name and usually a game mode. Every
property a playlist entry doesn't specify is inherited from the root of the configuration, so the root can
carry the common rules and an entry only override what makes its round different --- see
@ref Jazz2::Multiplayer::PlaylistEntry for the properties an entry may contain. The entry that is played
first is @cpp "PlaylistIndex" @ce, and once a round ends, the server advances to the next entry, wrapping
around at the end. With @cpp "RandomizePlaylist" @ce the list is shuffled when the server starts and again
on every wrap.

@m_class{m-noindent}

The playlist is also the only way for a dedicated server to know which level to load at all
@m_span{m-text m-dim} (see @ref multiplayer-hosting-dedicated) @m_endspan. A server hosted from the menu
uses it only when the level selection is left at the playlist, otherwise @cpp "PlaylistIndex" @ce is
@cpp -1 @ce and the chosen level is played until an admin changes it.

@subsection multiplayer-config-access Admins, whitelist and bans

Players are identified by a **Unique Player ID**, which every installation generates for itself. A player
finds their own in *Options* → *User Profile* @m_span{m-text m-dim} (the item copies it to the clipboard)
@m_endspan, and an admin can look up a connected one with the `/ip` command. Four properties of the
configuration take these IDs:

Property | Effect
-------- | ------
@cpp "AdminUniquePlayerIDs" @ce | Players in this map may use the admin commands. The value is reserved for a list of privileges, @cpp "*" @ce meaning all of them
@cpp "WhitelistedUniquePlayerIDs" @ce | If the map is not empty, **only** the players in it can join. The value is a user-defined comment
@cpp "BannedUniquePlayerIDs" @ce | Players in this map are rejected. The value is a user-defined comment, e.g. the reason
@cpp "BannedIPAddresses" @ce | The same for addresses, for a player whose ID is not known

@m_class{m-noindent}

@cpp "ServerPassword" @ce is the simpler alternative to a whitelist --- the server is visible to everybody,
but only a player who knows the password can enter it. @cpp "RequiresDiscordAuth" @ce additionally requires
a running **Discord** client, which limits the server to players on Linux, macOS and Windows.
@cpp "AllowCheats" @ce is off by default; when it's on, admins may use cheats in any game mode and other
players only in Cooperation, and always only on themselves.

@subsection multiplayer-config-reload Applying changes

The configuration file is read when the server starts. An admin can reload it at runtime with the
`/refresh` command, which re-reads the file and re-applies the welcome message, the game mode and the
current playlist entry, so most changes don't require a restart. Individual properties can also be changed
temporarily with `/set` @m_span{m-text m-dim} (see @ref multiplayer-commands) @m_endspan --- those changes
live only until the next `/refresh` or restart, they are never written back to the file.

@section multiplayer-visibility Public and private servers

Whether a server announces itself is decided by the @cpp "IsPrivate" @ce property
@m_span{m-text m-dim} (or by the menu item the host started it with) @m_endspan:

Behavior | Public server | Private server
-------- | ------------- | --------------
Announced on the local network | Yes, answers discovery requests on UDP port 7439 | No
Published to the online server list | Yes, to `https://de4th.dev/jazz2/servers` | No
Shown in **Join Server** | Yes, in both lists | No, has to be entered by address
Requires a reachable address | Yes, otherwise nobody can connect | Yes, for everyone who is given the address
Password, whitelist and bans | Apply the same way | Apply the same way

@m_class{m-noindent}

A private server runs no discovery at all, which is what makes it private: it appears in neither list, and
the only way in is its address. It is *not* an access restriction on its own --- anyone who learns the
address can connect --- so a server that should stay among friends is usually a private server **and** a
password-protected one.

@m_class{m-noindent}

A public server re-publishes itself to the online list every 5 minutes and delists itself when it shuts
down cleanly. The address it publishes is the one it detects for itself, which is of no use when the server
is behind NAT or in a container --- @cpp "ServerAddressOverride" @ce then specifies the address (and, if it
differs, the port) players on the internet should use. The port itself has to be reachable in any case:
@cpp "ServerPort" @ce over **UDP** for the default transport, and @cpp "WsPort" @ce over **TCP** if the
WebSocket transport is enabled. The `/endpoints` command lists the endpoints the server knows about,
including the override.

@section multiplayer-joining Joining a server

Choose **Play Multiplayer** → **Join Server** in the main menu. The list is filled from two sources at once
--- servers discovered on the local network and the public online list --- and shows the name, the player
count, the version and the address of each of them, marked with @cpp "ws" @ce for a server offering the
WebSocket transport, @cpp "R" @ce for reforged gameplay and @cpp "^" @ce for one found on the local
network. A version the client cannot join is highlighted. A server can also be entered by address, which
is the only way to reach a private server.

@m_class{m-noindent}

An endpoint is an IPv4 address, an IPv6 address in brackets or a domain name, optionally followed by a port
@m_span{m-text m-dim} (@cpp 7438 @ce is used when none is given) @m_endspan, or a `ws://` / `wss://` URL to
connect over the WebSocket transport:

@code{.sh}
192.168.1.10          # Local network, default port
example.com:7438      # Domain name and an explicit port
[2001:db8::1]:7438    # IPv6 address
wss://example.com:443 # WebSocket transport (browsers always use this one)
@endcode

@m_class{m-noindent}

The game can also connect right after it starts, without going through the menu, which is what a server
browser or a shortcut would use:

@code{.sh}
./jazz2 /connect example.com:7438 secret123
@endcode

@m_class{m-noindent}

The second argument is the endpoint and the optional third one is the server password. On joining, the
client and the server first check that their protocol versions match @m_span{m-text m-dim} (the patch part
is ignored, so only compatible releases are refused) @m_endspan, then the player is authenticated against
the whitelist, the ban lists and the password, and finally the client is asked for the assets of the level.
Anything it is missing --- levels, tilesets, music --- is downloaded from the server if the server allows
it with @cpp "AllowAssetStreaming" @ce; otherwise the player has to install the missing files manually.
Once in, an in-game lobby offers the character selection and the welcome message of the server.

@m_class{m-noindent}

If the connection drops, reconnecting within @cpp "ReconnectWindowSecs" @ce restores the progression of the
player --- weapons, lives, score, gems and championship points --- instead of starting over.

@section multiplayer-commands Server commands

Commands are typed into the in-game console @m_span{m-text m-dim} (the *backquote* or **T** key by default)
@m_endspan or, on a dedicated server, directly into its standard input. Anything that isn't a command is a
chat message. These are available to every player:

Command | Description
------- | -----------
`/info` | Name of the server, current level and game mode, player count, load and uptime
`/players` | List of connected players with their ping, points, kills, deaths and idle time
`/team <team>` | Requests a change to the `blue`, `red`, `green` or `yellow` team, or back to `auto`, if the server allows it in the current mode
`/kill` | Kills your own player
`/vote <type>` | Starts a poll to `restart` the playlist, `skip` the current level or `reset points`. It stays open for 60 seconds
`/yes` | Votes in the active poll

@m_class{m-noindent}

The remaining commands require admin rights, i.e. an entry in @cpp "AdminUniquePlayerIDs" @ce
@m_span{m-text m-dim} (the console of a dedicated server is always an admin) @m_endspan:

Command | Description
------- | -----------
`/set mode <mode>` | Changes the game mode, which restarts the round. Accepts the same values as the @cpp "GameMode" @ce property
`/set level <level>` | Loads another level and leaves the playlist
`/set name <name>` | Renames the server. An empty name stops publishing it to the online list
`/set welcome <message>` | Changes the welcome message of the in-game lobby, `\n` starts a new line
`/set kills`, `/set laps`, `/set treasure <n>` | Changes the win target of the current round
`/set teamcount <2-4>` | Changes the number of teams, which re-splits them and restarts the round
`/set autobalance`, `/set teamselect`, `/set friendlyfire <on/off>` | Team rules of the current round
`/set cheats <on/off>` | Allows or forbids cheats
`/set spawning <on/off>` | Stops or resumes spawning of players
`/next` | Ends the current round and continues with the next one
`/playlist [<index>]` | Jumps to a playlist entry, or advances to the next one
`/skip` | Skips the remaining pre-game countdown
`/balance` | Rebalances the teams immediately
`/setteam <player#> <team>` | Moves a player to a team
`/reset points` | Resets the championship points of all players
`/alert <message>` | Shows a message over the HUD of every player
`/kick <player>` | Disconnects a player, by name or by `#` and their index
`/kill <player>` | Kills a player without counting it as a death
`/ip <player>` | Address and Unique Player ID of a player
`/endpoints` | Endpoints the server is reachable at, including @cpp "ServerAddressOverride" @ce
`/refresh` | Reloads the configuration file, see @ref multiplayer-config-reload
`/vote cancel` | Cancels the active poll
`/exit`, `/quit` | Shuts the dedicated server down @m_span{m-text m-dim} (its own console only) @m_endspan

@section multiplayer-args Command-line arguments

Argument | Description
-------- | -----------
`/server [<config>]` | Runs the application as a dedicated server, see @ref multiplayer-hosting-dedicated. `--server` is accepted as well
`/connect <endpoint> [<password>]` | Connects to a server right after the start instead of showing the main menu. `--connect` and `-c` are accepted as well
`/config <path>` | Path to the preferences file, which is also the directory a relative server configuration path is resolved against
`/log` | Attaches a console to the process @m_span{m-text m-dim} (**Windows** only, where a non-server build has none) @m_endspan
`/log:file[:<name>]` | Writes the trace log into a file in the preferences directory, @cpp "Jazz2.log" @ce if no name is given

@m_class{m-noindent}

Opening the log file discards what the previous session wrote into it, so that content is first appended to
@cpp "Jazz2.log.gz" @ce next to it --- a plain **gzip** file holding one member per session, oldest first,
which any **gzip** tool reads back as one file with the sessions in order. It is trimmed from the front once
it outgrows 4 MB or 32 sessions, so the most recent sessions are always the ones kept. A log file named
explicitly with @cpp "/log:file:<name>" @ce is left as the single file it was asked for and gets no archive.

`/server` is available on **Linux** and **macOS**, and on **Windows** only in a debug build --- a release
server for Windows is the `DEDICATED_SERVER` build, where no argument is needed to become one and the first
argument is the path to the configuration file. In a `DEDICATED_SERVER` build the arguments of a client are
naturally not available either.

*/

}
